#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092376 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092113 :question/id #uuid "5bae6269-2112-56ea-b123-bee58701cf87" :question/math-academy-id "e-7130" :question/problem "Find the expression that gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t, \\, y=\\ln{(\\cos{t})}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (see graph below).\n\n![](images/48/48de26c11ae08afb45ec19ee56e770f7418f9be2afb8ee7dd46a0d1136cb125b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191 :topic/math-academy-id 1115 :topic/title "Selecting Procedures for Calculating Derivatives"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488 :topic/math-academy-id 207 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7208bd-8759-509f-aec8-b72d63dc377b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{2\\sin^{2} (4t) + \\cos^{2} (t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089610} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e4a462-def2-5041-9d86-629d00d6c6f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{\\sin^{2} (4t) + \\cos^{2} (t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089611} {:answer/id #uuid "e3499abe-efbf-509e-ba00-61325a39091c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{2\\cos^{2} (4t) + \\sin^{2} (t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089612} {:answer/id #uuid "e87d0171-bd01-58f1-a2a4-2a22124be105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} (4t) + \\sin^{2} (t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089613} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf170f4-362e-577e-893f-a65fc8d4acae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} (4t) + 2\\sin^{2} (t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089614}] :answer-field/id #uuid "13fc5e26-b2f3-5efb-bf53-9cf5f0c83d92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089609}] :question/id #uuid "2ac73185-5f28-5b9e-aeb9-15f1142ceee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-15155" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 1 + \\sin (4t), y = 4\\cos (t)$\nfrom $t = 0$ to $t = 2$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c02db39-1323-5178-823d-035cbc0e974b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273319} {:answer/id #uuid "76f538de-df54-5fad-9a26-4e6bcab7eb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273399} {:answer/id #uuid "7f93ecb1-ea78-5b92-86af-7eaf211452f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273483} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a2a416-fa2b-57e0-ae21-9414bcd449ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273807} {:answer/id #uuid "ebffd150-b312-515d-a26e-013ddad9564b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebffd150-b312-515d-a26e-013ddad9564b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274472} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d36b979-5ca7-502e-b3f6-581c418d1ffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274874}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d674e5dc-8a57-5368-b53e-8363dba234a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-15185" :question/problem "![](images/80/80577a585741748702b61596db84983592ecbc6c01fca78af033bf82dbe62ad2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=-3$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f016b0-2fd0-50db-a608-6fb63624e44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+16{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272994} {:answer/id #uuid "69d385f0-e607-521b-a6d5-ff44ee22ff0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+16{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273301} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8a87c9-2f3a-5822-a579-01d6eb0bbb36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273473} {:answer/id #uuid "8df9ee48-5fdc-55b9-9efb-127f8dd6bac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273611} {:answer/id #uuid "ad71957f-f727-5cbc-9851-57e268064b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e8a87c9-2f3a-5822-a579-01d6eb0bbb36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273473} :answer-field/id #uuid "18bf7715-0000-5c6a-ac8b-11821a5550c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b418c2bf-fd1a-57e4-845f-5c5fb4e1bb93" :question/math-academy-id "q-15163" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\nfrom $t=2$ to $t=5$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042ce01d-341b-5a8f-b26c-2bae1091ffbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284821} {:answer/id #uuid "085052cd-1f3e-5f31-9ff8-08a93c8512b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284852} {:answer/id #uuid "2277789f-926a-5240-a07d-b7aedc65a771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285096} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8848d4-7288-585c-b944-73aa40d45c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286635} {:answer/id #uuid "d0070ef7-3de9-5565-a190-4c165235a08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "085052cd-1f3e-5f31-9ff8-08a93c8512b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284852} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0b9c64d-9e93-55fe-a0fb-bcc5981e0b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287575}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "809a8d30-1507-55ed-ad1d-5fa778afd784" :question/math-academy-id "q-20909" :question/problem "![](images/59/59704c6b8e643337113ab99b22eaeaac8d59ac3c15042409e8a0a8eac6ee54fd.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y={e}^{t}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{({e}^{2t})+(\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1008f91c-5312-57ff-b584-94eee41fe74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288152} {:answer/id #uuid "1da60212-9b1b-5f90-b4ad-60a5d028fa99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288219} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0f56b4-96f6-5e10-b8c1-e96fcb0adb3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288328} {:answer/id #uuid "c400b002-970a-5f9c-b980-7c4fcc67cff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289327} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b052e5-81af-5157-9a69-881e064e72dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b052e5-81af-5157-9a69-881e064e72dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289641} :answer-field/id #uuid "92070a66-23e2-56dd-9cc9-c4b8a5e22afc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290057}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6e26edb-f404-57aa-9e8d-fef8eb7ecc78" :question/math-academy-id "q-21339" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d3de1db-0fcc-5d54-a34d-c4772f63450c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288331} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6783bc-60cb-5ebf-aed7-e258b2417e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288571} {:answer/id #uuid "72dafc8f-1319-50ac-8575-b91418fddf5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+3{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288830} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd6ac01-4a42-5fe6-a775-9f769a7e8298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+3{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288884} {:answer/id #uuid "8bef632c-9e9e-5375-8c17-7ff1526dfe34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bef632c-9e9e-5375-8c17-7ff1526dfe34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288967} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb71b35-5d29-5e3f-a2c7-defaa9483e1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289861}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97f4a57c-d78d-5eaa-81d0-22dc3ac7dd99" :question/math-academy-id "q-21347" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\nfrom $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69475fb9-4639-5aee-af40-ad859a263a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288774} {:answer/id #uuid "91284f31-5e28-57a6-85bb-2b392b818d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289008} {:answer/id #uuid "a96e4ea4-d995-5d4a-b26e-2d37c11a1916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289155} {:answer/id #uuid "ac501491-2831-575c-ac89-af59b5aa9473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289176} {:answer/id #uuid "ef891aab-5620-5243-8798-2c0bd3ae8835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac501491-2831-575c-ac89-af59b5aa9473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289176} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6962ab-6088-50b4-b289-81a33c131c7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289765}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1524414a-eeb0-5a99-93da-15745003f6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-21354" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95a19ab2-d61e-5cd4-9296-0ccc2a2ce9cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+32}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400081} {:answer/id #uuid "6f516eb5-80db-56b9-88c4-8fec44175002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400082} {:answer/id #uuid "d04936f6-e3a3-5955-87a3-ae992e7c288a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400083} {:answer/id #uuid "25a5d7fc-f3f7-50d4-9122-01398e9fb385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400084} {:answer/id #uuid "4e16c553-ec8a-5568-b27c-e4f4689e3032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e16c553-ec8a-5568-b27c-e4f4689e3032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400085} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bd27ca-b51f-55c8-a2b1-03c787e77a4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400080}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a37e6009-e864-56bc-93ae-b166a2b05514" :question/math-academy-id "q-123027" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=8-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(8-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+64-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡t-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+64}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221b2a78-3ed9-5fcc-a904-b72ed2d8cda6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400088} {:answer/id #uuid "2037ceca-da9b-5e6c-99af-f9e9635c71f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+6{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400089} {:answer/id #uuid "f6282b39-25dd-527c-8c08-c5ccbd4d5888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+12{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400090} {:answer/id #uuid "3954ccd4-113c-522e-8865-af8dedf5924f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400091} {:answer/id #uuid "5f536300-dcae-55c0-9423-568faa002b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f536300-dcae-55c0-9423-568faa002b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400092} :answer-field/id #uuid "065d677c-9998-56e4-8b57-ce841d04bf36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400087}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30eadd5a-469c-54ec-b683-aec3a996fde4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123030" :question/problem "![](images/88/8868658c9bc7195f435a0109fce26b8a4fa39324c0a9a39e3fb644c5f02cdc9b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b85309c-59d7-5810-84c4-7ea68f827ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401043} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd04e4b-26ba-5e0d-9091-43c5f06e6bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401044} {:answer/id #uuid "eb350998-6cb3-5e21-b128-0e21ffcf76a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401045} {:answer/id #uuid "9f297950-b005-5b22-b845-7190b859cf3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401046} {:answer/id #uuid "a99448ba-c640-5e57-a298-7dcd50a71e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb350998-6cb3-5e21-b128-0e21ffcf76a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401045} :answer-field/id #uuid "9286f2bc-6874-57c4-a822-f4b4566f80e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401042}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a2f2890-829e-5660-aa0e-71dea52c1f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-15178" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca290e8fd51edddf4381ec267b8e0a5692d0e52bd2a36b7d43ab89cbeb5cf00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c46a2e-4e00-5991-99c9-9dc68c9a9598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401050} {:answer/id #uuid "2499ce46-f979-5a96-92be-fd0c7ac31bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401051} {:answer/id #uuid "61eb1149-6ab5-5e3a-8377-404854e1b082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401052} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed903c6-dd0e-5b89-95d6-7cb6b868a842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401053} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b764bb-1a8d-5c44-bf30-3132c5443550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2499ce46-f979-5a96-92be-fd0c7ac31bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401051} :answer-field/id #uuid "d340f787-388c-5296-b3a4-86e39060db0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401049}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "243b404d-a4f2-57e8-bc56-d916439edf0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15182" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d683c3960705fa32d11826008a9844c51c8e59e38f5158fce7f4e44c31b97ca9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a58988e-9e60-53e1-9ccc-6195ac2bdc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401868} {:answer/id #uuid "c4824b8b-2ed7-5c3c-ae44-9e7e39c2adaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401869} {:answer/id #uuid "8e755153-eb7c-5c80-ac13-6bd2f55ebc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401870} {:answer/id #uuid "8dadc22b-b156-5944-b045-f3c498f81787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401871} {:answer/id #uuid "89771be0-5923-5fe1-8923-30d66256f236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89771be0-5923-5fe1-8923-30d66256f236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401872} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d97d84-3165-5293-95f8-8ad9ae1b81ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401867}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a60afb71-a1f2-5589-86e8-84aaf38f221a" :question/math-academy-id "q-21353" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4aa2bca8faab077c47fb968882579233babe7d323ba3120088e8ea5599fbd570.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={e}^{t},\\,y=t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110457 :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/math-academy-id "e-3482" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. \n\nSo our final answer is $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291 :topic/math-academy-id 112 :topic/title "Properties of the Unit Circle in the First Quadrant"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2b084c-2030-569c-b3db-c1f0bb240497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108674} {:answer/id #uuid "a1178c63-4702-5bdc-9e41-c3046acb6f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108675} {:answer/id #uuid "d3dca092-57eb-5e07-8d0d-0d53376fcddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108676} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1a8afd-47ad-5ee5-aef2-253b8da8405f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108677} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b6b5a3-b768-5bb1-8d7e-9d7a7b3b6af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108678}] :answer-field/id #uuid "af420e31-aeb4-5e79-ae37-c4b53833e453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108673}] :question/id #uuid "6d39d8f8-c6eb-55ec-8766-ad9675623f6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37821" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d51a8e78f8b8c1f05e502bd98aed6a253799336765f1331d23e63ec637bcb448.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin θ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218611