2186257665} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc46843-1ec1-59a1-bcce-8c1afc2c6e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258046} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cbcfd0-4ae8-5672-951c-dc2e216fa8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6cbcfd0-4ae8-5672-951c-dc2e216fa8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258238} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2385214-bc0d-583b-b908-7acf965a5700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259317}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "371bdeca-3a35-5956-9fa6-e8d03505d26b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124625" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e50a755a20330d7f0eca8d02c8ddab33f392d5b70f7ca35b4dc514e22e61141c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{36}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4cafde-db4c-54bb-bab3-013f762c6d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321731} {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf875a9-930b-5577-b821-7ead99c40d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321884} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe73b65-ebc3-5cff-9a52-a69da7741fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322150} {:answer/id #uuid "b124652f-7ab9-5b4c-9413-d3dfdcb4c6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce29bc07-9e48-58fd-9669-3eeb517f79e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "565688d3-a42b-5b17-9265-a372de4b8754" :question/math-academy-id "q-37899" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe point $P$ lies on the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{3}{7},$ use the Pythagorean Identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{3}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8126b5-2054-5d88-865f-be6cbe674d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321368} {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "51eee8c3-23ea-5613-a452-4c2182222cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4b8d7-052a-5cf2-86db-50636fe22d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321871} {:answer/id #uuid "c2131ada-4ea2-5f29-85f1-aa42dc442a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} :answer-field/id #uuid "617b107f-dc16-5899-aab5-ab0f5850cc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323364}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9112180a-ac6b-5472-ba8b-819d346ead1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d900898-f644-59cb-8bd9-292f8cf96161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333108} {:answer/id #uuid "128843bf-c0d0-5b0b-9af7-5c0891c02535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333155} {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d463a-06e0-5a98-b913-8a3a09131403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334926} {:answer/id #uuid "f58082e0-41ed-5179-bfb1-b391fbd71bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4eb5247-b68b-5a82-8180-4c0ffe3de0aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335575}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9c7086f-08af-5d07-8de2-8cc3da9eb077" :question/math-academy-id "q-64681" :question/problem "![](images/94/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebb8345-8448-5af3-a7b5-f875fc57e26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333119} {:answer/id #uuid "87f8bdf1-b693-5449-95dc-41601ab98dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334192} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4a426-2676-5c7e-b66d-c6dd8478bed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334369} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334503} {:answer/id #uuid "af99c88e-1b1e-54e6-8e94-cc6dcf9705d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334503} :answer-field/id #uuid "2831461c-74ea-58e4-8c19-3e42bd31b998" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335313}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43fa1511-b2d2-52bf-92cb-53a58f4a78b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-65059" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d292a3598346bd05511c9c8979ba42845a88afe75a4740d8c4bdb9aa3968c87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/48/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242d899f622c43010662e28edcabe0e1bde3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343c6fe7-74c0-52a2-bc86-d208dbf32b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511689} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5a672c-e194-5807-a342-905cf49227f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511693} {:answer/id #uuid "75399b50-a49f-5812-89c2-ed1173a8611d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511696} {:answer/id #uuid "c39a656b-c2c8-5877-9d30-c0d08addf19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511706} {:answer/id #uuid "ead3e7bc-0d8f-5814-a629-e38097f034cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "343c6fe7-74c0-52a2-bc86-d208dbf32b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\fra