$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{6}{25}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{38}{25}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b266fd5-0108-5d1b-b55c-e7ab7da30986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511682} {:answer/id #uuid "3231f0e8-0e93-53ea-825c-432c55cfd011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511688} {:answer/id #uuid "38075e8b-f56a-5110-8581-0f19e65d1cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511690} {:answer/id #uuid "6c632c64-803b-5920-ba44-0bd15d2842da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511695} {:answer/id #uuid "a47751bb-fbec-549f-b413-287c65e22ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38075e8b-f56a-5110-8581-0f19e65d1cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511690} :answer-field/id #uuid "85df7c4c-736c-5b18-b67c-bc225c6084a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511697}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e339c15-6319-50b0-b160-b0fbf1d94d58" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0cdc84ef38e2c2074bdab95458a97452209858b74a370b65bd7f393c1bde908.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69ab1b2e23a2815343db2b914204aa5864e73254dd1aa22d501c08b8ae155714.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{17}{49}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{34}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{64}{49}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037cbbc0-15eb-5ca5-9687-929e9c03c3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511681} {:answer/id #uuid "5524e754-213c-5297-b6c8-38ca120bb015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511692} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc4b2b4-ef85-5136-ae4f-475a2d6e7ccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511698} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1ea995-d6ac-507a-b61c-f3087c637df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511699} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2e1cc1-cb15-5e19-b2ed-06cfc90ef286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd2e1cc1-cb15-5e19-b2ed-06cfc90ef286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511714} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa6be0a-2bd9-5771-ae42-ec9c7d256501" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511684}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba9e8819-628f-5c44-89ce-c66c0188feda" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd7cf164aa9ac00697292aabf03d616b62b216014ac00a0f36e9194ad7685ce2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/df/df6444faa947e137efc95beeb24c59e8e685830096bf4019fef776abd87c4bff.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{6}{36}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02858282-63f7-5705-8f8e-00f513fcf0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511680} {:answer/id #uuid "b84e32c1-da66-5b76-9568-280c26d49655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511704} {:answer/id #uuid "d39f7a6b-0199-5e05-a5c4-d51a6cc4d9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511707} {:answer/id #uuid "f13958bf-0d8f-555a-bf51-2bf79723a49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511711} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd43435-b072-5f01-b302-1d59e6fd5742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbd43435-b072-5f01-b302-1d59e6fd5742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511713} :answer-field/id #uuid "21996560-b703-53d3-bfc5-aac31475eee5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511685}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd4c56b2-c037-5749-b1cc-f43f2eb74d1e" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a35a31ccce6ce9e50c386b920c8c4b0fecf23a4f29741e982dcd6c69bf38453c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/09/0961629419accd50af36ce88aecafe33ea264d278d18d6f71c4aa4d76c06be97.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{13}{25}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{26}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{24}{25}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110459 :question/id #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3485" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5},$ what is $\\tan\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d58f0d3a-48cb-5dc6-87a4-afe1cfebcbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109760} {:answer/id #uuid "db8dde9f-3c21-548d-8ee7-573fad3133d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/re