owing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}{(\\frac{8}{9})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "249f6d39-c352-5b6e-b0a3-aad8dbe4af0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510728} {:answer/id #uuid "40e4413d-62c5-5cf3-8a6d-bc0951e1d6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{83}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510731} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae139e3-a55e-5972-9da7-3d735e750073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510738} {:answer/id #uuid "cf16047a-f770-52e9-8ba0-da97bd2d6088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510745} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa164a1-1ef9-5a0d-9231-f146dcc770a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf16047a-f770-52e9-8ba0-da97bd2d6088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510745} :answer-field/id #uuid "af481ffe-765a-562a-87bb-3829101afbf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2014768a-a233-5eee-b9c5-afc2675420b0" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccdb5bb029197875aaeebfc899dff4d25597b657e64ea95d1b34d09df0a37789.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af413350ef3c9edde0eebf49cb8f37c8b20791f4bb01e04adaacaa0c378d28a1.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{43}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43\\cdot 6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1909811c-da8d-539b-aa89-cdc5b420a83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510725} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc3517e-1300-5538-b247-d948b579f7f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510729} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9189a3-d0b3-5483-9c84-198750079c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510748} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b2d445-8bcf-5fd5-a74d-dc7c7c42cc23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510750} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e48c42-ff53-5bcc-b008-e6ccbde37ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b2d445-8bcf-5fd5-a74d-dc7c7c42cc23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510750} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c54992-c621-521f-8066-259a4e4e6c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510742}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c08d8db-de56-5ccc-8319-0573cd7cc4f0" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d04fe72eb2ad53b69837aaf402addd75d9b087225d70e876233e23092fc85764.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/51/51778a4029b91dbbd51014a8f4a3e7e1fc772efe96058f41b21bbf408f113cce.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{18}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18\\cdot 7}}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{126}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cd8750-e5e7-52ce-b115-cbd111d3d902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510726} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba5953e-1bc3-52b3-949b-b1a3372ec514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510734} {:answer/id #uuid "dca87888-b247-5218-bd97-b52279791479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510746} {:answer/id #uuid "ed349bbe-0002-555d-b774-e4baa909f28c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{79}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510749} {:answer/id #uuid "f51c1080-9d67-5d5c-9ad2-f5bee4c4e810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ba5953e-1bc3-52b3-949b-b1a3372ec514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510734} :answer-field/id #uuid "89a72e76-dfbf-51a2-96d4-d8c440244cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510736}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55b50355-dd9b-57b2-ba6b-ec0a86439c05" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2c66fc4d5fa2ac5b2d6263764cf1eaa4e845680561833c5bfa46743df3408d2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af1047be250d877d836851da681e583ada8f01d2022e5c9671aa83173ed23fa3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{39}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39\\cdot 10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc4984a-76a7-5e40-bec6-4baeb697eb93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510732} {:answer/id #uuid "93b2d161-28c4-50ff-962d-90ee2fbdac17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510737} {:answer/id #uuid "a332604f-d236-5a81-8925-b96c1993b9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510739} {:answer/id #uuid "b382a9d0-fd0f-530d-8c12-c535c8651899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510741} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc011f-2ad6-5dc2-af67-11ebde52edbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93b2d161-28c4-50ff-962d-90ee2fbdac17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510737} :answer-field/id #uuid "68af0948-d377-54ba-bc95-3bf16b5f82fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510735}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cab2c352-408a-5716-9c82-d09e82069fc3" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd9061a8ef1b2f0d8fe9d730f18c5e10bef42a9acd33f23d36efb3728ce0614b.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{15}{17},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ba/bad705982f073623644ed540e26cfad849b2dd078c5c64772a0be3937d618a27.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{15}{17}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{225}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{17}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{15}{17})}{(\\frac{8}{17})}=\\frac{15}{8}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124242 :question/id #uuid "70bcf628-cf1f-5da0-85b7-19d0f514ccd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3069" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635 :topic/math-academy-id 418 :topic/title "Equations of Lines in Point-Slope Form"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0315c355-94e8-5b85-bc0d-c26e707e74cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{4}{5}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321176} {:answer/id #uuid "9db2c19a-f9cc-5cd6-8a22-46dc603af896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322389} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f0e-14b9-56f8-a702-fc4364be3a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322605} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c279f3-89f6-5f4b-b92c-736849d41a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322911} {:answer/id #uuid "f880ea2c-47c4-55a4-afa7-5ab8c96b72f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{2}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9db2c19a-f9cc-5cd6-8a22-46dc603af896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322389} :answer-field/id #uuid "36f4a259-af06-543c-939e-9833986d33ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323278}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "491a90b9-f33c-5937-8491-8e8447d7cf82" :question/math-academy-id "q-36219" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{6xy+{y}^{2}}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =\\frac{3(1)+(2)}{\\sqrt{6(1)(2)+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{5}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd26c8b-fb1c-5766-9fbe-ab3b0f2403f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2-2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514935} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbc826e-b561-565f-a693-c12cb77c5362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515003} {:answer/id #uuid "940c73e1-fac8-5d96-a4d7-4f31a5f433a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515186} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab403d6-b2c8-5db7-ac14-47e28ec612f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515199} {:answer/id #uuid "d540c582-ba40-5073-81c9-81443650b392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1, \\\\ z=4x-4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "940c73e1-fac8-5d96-a4d7-4f31a5f433a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515186} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec17033d-7e32-53b3-b00f-f4c5e370a468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385351}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "faf15e19-a012-50bb-842f-791131393cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36369" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3x+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)} & =3(1)+6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =3(x-1) \\\\ z & =3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106c62f5-6a15-5356-9819-bac29ddd8e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266282} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdcc8ed-799e-5868-8d9c-e5cddb211a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266824} {:answer/id #uuid "502e00e2-2746-5fc9-8240-98511ddcf672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=2x+9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266856} {:answer/id #uuid "50dcaed5-8a14-56a6-9c8d-e4ff85b79e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-2x+15\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266866} {:answer/id #uuid "9b80ced7-aa68-5bf4-8dc4-6402f10538d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=2x+21\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cdcc8ed-799e-5868-8d9c-e5cddb211a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266824} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d228b2-8aea-57a5-b2ca-483e7c7b3e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268529}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "484f8b36-97b4-5e4f-a875-d05f61c48bf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132585" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Given that the point $P(4,-2,5)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y\\sqrt{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(4,-2,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(4,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)} & =(-2)\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(4,-2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}(x-4) \\\\ z-5 & =-4(x-4) \\\\ z & =-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,-2,5)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0839792e-52f8-55c0-96a0-793207bc2a23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266218} {:answer/id #uuid "1f23aed8-c0d3-5c83-bb76-cf7ec415c01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266413} {:answer/id #uuid "af853526-8839-56ef-bbab-ba5bdb85abf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267724} {:answer/id #uuid "d3488bb1-cb08-55c3-baf7-71b2ba5e7513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268028} {:answer/id #uuid "e94121d6-a767-5df0-af24-04d3cf8908a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0839792e-52f8-55c0-96a0-793207bc2a23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266218} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc73d7a-8739-5705-bbef-4b94db05673c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c434702-618c-5a71-8a4a-aadc6bd7fee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132587" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2xy+2$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)(2)+2 \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =6(x-1) \\\\ z & =6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3535f187-54bf-56fb-8443-7dab32a9c79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425099} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c97aa-3243-5467-ac12-075c53d9654e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425100} {:answer/id #uuid "43046f4c-8850-5dea-9f71-c5e3ebec2500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425101} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8e160b-491c-5c8e-996d-6c8ececaab38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425102} {:answer/id #uuid "77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425106} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab8e018-d349-5c74-b2e8-1930a7b5eded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268476}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c5dd738-1c94-548e-aa53-3b5d32dc4abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132589" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b49c0a3-842a-5e4f-8c9f-bcdacd6482db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x+2\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266525} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa233f4-64d9-5e7c-9bf4-5d6de3431a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=2x-2\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266718} {:answer/id #uuid "5722d450-4de6-5584-a7a4-4c5a28d785ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266918} {:answer/id #uuid "8af7c44c-aec2-5a26-b0b4-846d06ad93e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267389} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f58a5e-b636-567f-a4e0-4e5efca66f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5722d450-4de6-5584-a7a4-4c5a28d785ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266918} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a7709cb-f264-5087-8141-9df67bddfa9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268475}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90718d28-70a4-5587-a36e-fa57ec8d26d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132590" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{xy}{\\pi }$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}(x-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ z-2\\pi  & =-2(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ z & =-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499732} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8f64a9-ff65-58b8-9065-4abeb8498b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499859} {:answer/id #uuid "8405b123-1df2-58c7-a42b-133a4390e156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499905} {:answer/id #uuid "89e9a579-0e60-5841-a0a0-a7fc520c336b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499923} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5f83b9-acd0-55f1-b0be-802d7971c9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499732} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e7d076-c660-5037-a4ff-af8a315c239c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268835}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22a41e65-3d40-52de-a0eb-a4c7353c0c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-132593" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d6e4604-362d-57a9-a29b-e6f955c20002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2}y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499720} {:answer/id #uuid "7344417b-833c-54ed-9d9c-2ee1f6469356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499866} {:answer/id #uuid "821cfb95-3293-5dd2-ae19-a95141c69336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{4}y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499901} {:answer/id #uuid "a05b6b84-89fc-51b8-b22e-5a5a84b42efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499964} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fb22f8-31af-560a-a251-6124b9e4d0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{2}y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7344417b-833c-54ed-9d9c-2ee1f6469356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499866} :answer-field/id #uuid "92a71cca-98d2-5245-a4d1-fd31406052e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268663}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac300975-45c9-5a2c-a8b5-5d2addf48a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-132595" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "204035e4-46d4-534d-ba05-15ce0e052f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321387} {:answer/id #uuid "5b70aad1-ccca-5779-affa-e5700fd0cc6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321868} {:answer/id #uuid "7510c1fa-11fc-530d-ac02-4cbbe0845097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322059} {:answer/id #uuid "85c6028a-d0f8-58ef-9cb9-0c6994f1659c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4x+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322206} {:answer/id #uuid "9cadbd18-60fa-56f2-aef5-823c9dd90a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7510c1fa-11fc-530d-ac02-4cbbe0845097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322059} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b4da25-e31a-52b9-9b49-54d616020d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323339}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daecb367-4754-591f-93db-eaa083b20ffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-36301" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,0,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+4y+x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,0,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)} & =(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-8 & =-4(y-0) \\\\ z & =-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,0,8)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a88646-da7b-5cc5-a6ca-e3694eb78578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321182} {:answer/id #uuid "31748a7a-7455-5dc9-aebc-92650c97c472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321526} {:answer/id #uuid "a14281d6-156f-5f48-beed-65acb59f3aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-12\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322421} {:answer/id #uuid "af9c34b4-7ca6-552a-a577-8a9187d131ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=-4y+12\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322525} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f4b2b7-738a-52b8-9ea5-229baa45ac6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0f4b2b7-738a-52b8-9ea5-229baa45ac6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b9822b-8388-5077-b4c1-9ebc7d355fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323295}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12d0b28e-9c11-5054-bcb2-feb86bd6fc4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36441" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Given that the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(0.5,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡((0.5)(2))+\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =4(y-2) \\\\ z & =4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0.5,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c5ba22e-dbcf-5fa2-95ae-a8ddaa66f720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=6y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425107} {:answer/id #uuid "8020f82d-21fe-5f0d-b435-1b1601809556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425108} {:answer/id #uuid "9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425110} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d9df8e-4012-5e27-bf02-9250e930e1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425111} {:answer/id #uuid "f37ea42d-7c61-5d99-8c24-718ac347ab47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425110} :answer-field/id #uuid "335b522d-b8a5-5ac7-8b9d-2985076f5c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338374}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3868b943-72a6-5a73-9952-d94521bce0ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-71209" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-1 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156f0e60-a655-5cee-ace2-00f94feeb9b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336234} {:answer/id #uuid "3998953b-84b0-5889-81f7-4dc1224db93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336573} {:answer/id #uuid "4603346d-02b6-5575-9939-64549e4f33fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336681} {:answer/id #uuid "6b358259-0538-5cb3-840e-3b6005519fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336981} {:answer/id #uuid "ef59035a-1a91-53fe-8a72-a5d6bc5d3dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3998953b-84b0-5889-81f7-4dc1224db93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336573} :answer-field/id #uuid "67c2ebb0-7f04-5390-9b3f-6c5b9ac02ee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338458}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceda561f-8269-5197-b05a-efc41af9a9a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71218" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df6297f-0710-55c1-aeca-d0bbd6193592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=5y+4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436940} {:answer/id #uuid "46868586-3cca-540f-a185-1309d5aab5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-14\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436942} {:answer/id #uuid "83e7b9f8-74d8-5b05-8940-fc35083f8350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x+6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436945} {:answer/id #uuid "891f8685-7212-51ca-9476-5b254f301d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=-5x-6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436946} {:answer/id #uuid "a94a09cf-cb2f-5cbc-9de1-d32e3178d6bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a94a09cf-cb2f-5cbc-9de1-d32e3178d6bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436947} :answer-field/id #uuid "2712fe89-2404-5ec9-a1bd-af26bb9d16b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391978}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "376329be-a379-553e-b714-516f831f127f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36372" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(2,0,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,0,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,0) & ={e}^{(0)}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,0)}(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,0,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f49be2f-9fbd-57d9-8743-c7f792e14929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407305} {:answer/id #uuid "40205211-9c82-5bbc-b29c-5211d1a45bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-2y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407306} {:answer/id #uuid "645649e7-f6ca-5dcf-a129-028646be2cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407308} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3f6706-e60e-5d15-b360-58385706ee4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-2y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407309} {:answer/id #uuid "d4018ef0-2dba-5e3f-8527-3ea960bf4de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4018ef0-2dba-5e3f-8527-3ea960bf4de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407311} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94262ee-f4dd-5ac5-a015-9c5cf4798aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407315}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05b78370-0b37-5800-aa28-6b6b17964b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-71216" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,0,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022e71c7-1d96-5b12-ab9b-3c65246aca6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499657} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecbc247-a7b4-51ad-ac21-7f50b73d7f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499803} {:answer/id #uuid "4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499806} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf544a9-31d0-5386-9054-024015e9e1e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=3x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500070} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fec6bc-c021-533e-ab85-5127fbbe71f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=2x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499806} :answer-field/id #uuid "1450b1c6-e44c-5c3f-b403-2b991fbf5f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500191}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac629eca-77ed-5bb7-99e7-49845bdc5d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71204" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(y+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0)\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10528982-819d-558d-b7b9-c606e71872b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=3x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514906} {:answer/id #uuid "7d86ddc1-2c7d-548c-9b05-047b8457b098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=x-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515131} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e89105-6501-5255-9bbc-617aef43299e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=x-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515228} {:answer/id #uuid "becf689a-2251-5072-95a8-008d1c2e4200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515277} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b5a29-3a47-5678-8b2a-9278853cd35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b5a29-3a47-5678-8b2a-9278853cd35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515373} :answer-field/id #uuid "37d670d1-3bff-5409-be4d-e79488473006" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515430}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae3534f1-44d3-55be-bcdb-7634e16c97c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132594" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2x{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )} & =2(\\frac{1}{2}){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311d0b20-5465-53bf-8af8-033f8a691b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-4 \\\\ z=- 8 x - 17\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518850} {:answer/id #uuid "4c90283e-17c2-5956-a222-1db0ed4abe72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-4 \\\\ z=15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518858} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5473db-40c3-50b6-8ddd-7b6cadb61d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-4 \\\\ z=16 - 8 x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518890} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b88192-c899-53c8-837d-bdb7ab15dfa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=- 8 y - 33\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518892} {:answer/id #uuid "da098dab-79f4-58a3-80e8-513f4358580d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-4 \\\\ z=8 x - 17\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c90283e-17c2-5956-a222-1db0ed4abe72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-4 \\\\ z=15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518858} :answer-field/id #uuid "e73da421-63d8-5f13-81f5-0f4c7d6bdcd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518897}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03efd4b4-041a-5564-818d-06a45aad1a76" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-4.$ Given that the point $P(2,-4,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=- 4 x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,-4,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-4) & =-4(2) \\\\ & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,-4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-8)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,-4,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-4 \\\\ z=15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cf7030-0c6b-58ec-b96b-cc1eaee16f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- \\pi \\\\ z=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518837} {:answer/id #uuid "2350b4de-0a61-567a-8e6e-86269a60e405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- \\pi \\\\ z=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518843} {:answer/id #uuid "a3381c9b-b21a-5c98-a1cc-92e5b24f891c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- \\pi \\\\ z=\\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518881} {:answer/id #uuid "bcef6f07-ae1f-515e-9823-933456ed0e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- \\pi \\\\ z=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x - 2\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518888} {:answer/id #uuid "eb70d200-f386-5ec6-b52c-68c76a65bcc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(y + \\pi\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2350b4de-0a61-567a-8e6e-86269a60e405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- \\pi \\\\ z=- \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right) + 9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518843} :answer-field/id #uuid "8840c5cb-d522-528d-9760-41089c81f54c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0bc337d9-050a-554a-8d77-360e1bc2538f" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Given that the point $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-2,- \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,- \\pi) & =(- \\pi)\\cos((-2)(- \\pi))+\\cos(- \\pi) \\\\ & =(- \\pi)(1)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =- \\pi - 1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(9) & =(- \\pi - 1)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z & =- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=- \\pi \\\\ z=- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c129fb-2de3-52fe-aa21-6bb037b5ed9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ z=6 y + 15\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518863} {:answer/id #uuid "7d447cec-93f4-5f7f-866b-3cc5460bed1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=6 x + 21\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518871} {:answer/id #uuid "7f84a2d7-a0be-5d0a-b257-029f139cfa5e" :answer/t